# FigDown — figures as text. Spec: https://github.com/FigDown/figdown

# Demonstrator for `flow left` (maintainer review): direction is
# a property of the FIGURE, and a response path is the natural subject for it
# — the request went rightward off-stage, and this figure shows the reply
# travelling back, so the flow axis runs right to left and the drawing reads
# the way the sentence does. One `flow` line per document; a second is a line
# error.
figdown 0.1 block
title "Response path — the reply travels right to left"

class reply "Response — travels opposite to the request path" stroke=#2563eb

node origin "Origin server"
node cache  "Edge cache"
node client "Client"

flow left

edge origin -[200 OK]-> cache class=reply
edge cache -[200 OK (cached copy)]-> client class=reply

# Layout zone — and the corpus's negative-coordinate demonstrator, moved here
# from the RPF figure when that one went fully automatic. `at=`
# is canvas px and the origin is a reference point, not a wall: the parser
# accepts a negative component and the layout honours it. Here it lifts the
# cache above the reply line, the way a side cache is drawn.
#
# WHAT A NEGATIVE COORDINATE DOES NOT DO — verified by rendering: it does not
# move the page. The canvas grows RIGHT and DOWN only, so a negative
# coordinate spends the margin that already exists above the content and
# nothing more; past that the element is CLIPPED, not merely misplaced (the
# layout gate's offcv axis reports exactly that). A negative x has no margin
# to spend at all and always clips.
layout
pin cache at=(210,-16)
